# Gold Leaf - a signwriter, a shopfront that is closing, and work that outlives its reason.
# Written for the library. One person, one craft, and the question of what a job is for when nobody is coming.

title: Gold Leaf
start: start

== start ==
bg east_corner
music room_tone
Forty years of lettering and you can still tell a 1950s fascia from a 1980s one across a
street: the weight of the strokes, where the serif sits, whether the man who did it was
paid by the letter or by the day.
The call came from the grocer at the end of the row, who is shutting in March, and who
wants her fascia repainting before she goes.
-> the_look

== the_look ==
You go and look at it on the Tuesday. It is a good fascia: hand-lettered in 1948 by a man
whose name is under the return at the left end in pencil, and the gold has gone to a sort
of grey-brown, and the black ground has crazed, and it is worth doing and it is worth doing
properly and the woman standing on the pavement telling you about the shop does not need to
be told either of those things.
-> the_ask

== the_ask ==
you: You know it'll be painted over. New people, or a shutter, or a skip.
her: I know.
you: So it's for six months.
her: It's for me. I've looked at that sign for fifty-one years and my father looked at it
before me and I'm not having it go grey on my watch. You can put that on the bill as a
line if you like.
-> the_knowing

== the_knowing ==
Two ways to do it. Gold leaf is real gold, beaten to a thousandth of a hair and laid onto
size with a gilder's tip, and it takes four days and the size has to be right to the hour or
the leaf goes dull, and it costs a hundred and forty in materials. Gold paint is a tin of
paint, and it takes an afternoon, and it looks nearly as good from across the road and
nothing like it from a yard away.
The weather is going to break on Thursday. You are sixty-three and your hands are still
steady in the morning and not in the afternoon.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
The fascia is four metres. The woman is on the pavement with her coat buttoned to the neck
and she has already said the sentence about the bill, which means she has thought about this
more than she has said.
* Do it in leaf. Four days, and do it right. -> leaf
* Do it in gold paint. An afternoon, and honest about it. -> paint
* Tell her what the four days cost and let her decide. -> the_price
* Tell her she is paying for something nobody will see. -> the_argue

== leaf ==
You lay the leaf on the Thursday and the Friday and the Saturday, and the size behaves on
two of the three, and on the Friday afternoon your hand is not what it was in the morning
and you stop and start again on the Saturday, which costs you a day and costs her nothing
because you had quoted four days and four days is what it took.
It is the best piece of work you have done since 2011. She comes out on the Sunday morning
to look at it in the flat light and stands there for a long time without saying anything,
which is the correct response and the one you get about twice a decade.
-> ending_leaf

== paint ==
You do it in an afternoon in gold paint, and you tell her exactly what it is, and she says
that is what she asked for when she said repainting, and she pays the smaller bill and
thanks you and means it.
It looks well from the other side of the road. Up close the strokes carry a slight ridge and
the gold is a colour rather than a metal, and for six months every day she opens that shop
she walks under it, and it is the right sign, and it is not the sign that was there.
-> ending_paint

== the_price ==
You tell her the two prices while standing on the pavement, in the cold, without a
clipboard, which is a thing you can do at sixty-three and could not at thirty.
She does not hesitate, which is what you expected, and then she says the other thing, which
is that she wants the man's name under the return kept in pencil, and that she does not want
it varnished over.
-> ending_named

== ending_named ==
The name under the return is a nuisance to keep: it is in pencil on the wood, it is under
where the new work goes, and the only way to keep it is to leave that end unpainted or to
transfer it, which is a thing signwriters do and which is not the same as keeping it.
You do it properly: you lift the pencil line onto the new ground in a way that will last,
and you write your own name under the other end, which you have never done, because there is
now a line of two of you on the same piece of wood.
-> END

== the_argue ==
you: It's six months of a thing nobody will look at.
her: I will.
you: You'll be looking at boxes and a van.
her: I'll be looking at it.
She says it in a flat way that leaves no room, and you have had this conversation before with
people whose reasons were worse, and you stop having it and pick the tools up out of the van,
which is the only honourable way to end an argument you have started.
-> the_decided

== the_decided ==
You lay the leaf. Four days, the weather holds, and it is the best work you have done since
2011 for reasons that have very little to do with the gold.
She shuts in March. The shutter goes on the Tuesday and the sign is behind it, and the shop
is a phone repair place by July and the fascia is a flat vinyl board, and you do not go and
look, because you know exactly what it looks like and you have a photograph.
-> END

== ending_leaf ==
The shop shuts in March and the sign goes behind a shutter and then behind a vinyl board in
July, and you do not know how long the wood is there under it, and you never will.
That is the whole of the trade and you have known it for forty years: you make things for
the wall they are on and the wall outlives the reason, and the sign was never for the street
or for the customers or for the next people. It was for a woman standing on a pavement in
February saying I'm not having it go grey on my watch. You have never once been asked for
anything better than that.
-> END

== ending_paint ==
You see her in the street the following year, after the shop, and she says hello and asks
about your hands, and neither of you mentions the sign, and that is not an omission: there
is nothing to say about an afternoon's work that did what it was asked to do.
It looked well from the other side of the road. She walked under it twice a day for six
months and it did not go grey on her watch, and that is what she asked for, and you gave her
exactly what she asked for, and you have thought about it more often than she has.
-> END
